I agree, most of the 'Learning the arts of game programming' books out
there either focus completely on Doom/Wolfenstein 3D game engines, or
are WAY too technical for the entry level programmer (although Andre
Lamothe's Teach Yourself Game Programming book, while being somewhat
outdated, does teach the intermediate programmer some pretty good
techniques). 
	The best method is to start from the ground up. Start off with maybe 'C
For Dummies' (and EXCELLENT starting point - highly recommended) from
IDG and a couple of Waite Group or SAMS books afterwards. Waite Group
books are overall easier and better written then the SAMS, however they
are somwhat bad about TYPOS (although very rare). 	One book that I would
highly recommend (after C or C++ for dummies) would be Master C++ for
Windows (from the Waite group.) The teaching is based on an online
course (from the included diskette) and the book is really a supplement
to the lesson (with review exercises, references, etc.) Most of the code
learned is portable to VC++, BC++, and DJGPP. 
